From: SM-X710 postmarketOS port <gts9wifi@example.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 04:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: stop logging a repeated handover interrupt

The ADSP handover interrupt is level-triggered and the remote processor keeps
it asserted after the first handover, so q6v5_handover_interrupt() fires again
and again for the rest of the session.  On this tablet it repeats about 5.4
times a second and accounts for 99.98 % of the kernel log ring, which makes
dmesg unusable for anything else -- including the debugging of every other
subsystem.

The repeat is expected and harmless: the handover has already been performed by
the time it arrives.  Log it at debug level so it cannot flood the log.  There
is no behaviour change; the interrupt is still handled the same way.

Signed-off-by: SM-X710 postmarketOS port <gts9wifi@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,14 @@
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * The handover interrupt is level-triggered and the remote keeps it
+		 * asserted after the first handover, so this fires again and again
+		 * (about five times a second on the SM-X710) and buries the rest of
+		 * the kernel log.  The handover itself has already happened, so log
+		 * the repeat at debug level instead of at error level.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
